Scott asserts that \(\lambda x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1}.\{x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1}\}:D^n\to\mathbb{P}D\) is approximable and computable when \(D\) is effectively given, that \(\lambda x.\{x\}\) exhibits \(D\trianglelefteq\mathbb{P}D\), and that \(\{x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1}\}=\{x_0\}\cap\cdots\cap\{x_{n-1}\}\); the neighbourhood test is \(X_k\) related to \(\bigcup_{i<q}(\uparrow X_{n_i})\) if and only if \(\exists i<q.\, X_k\sqsubseteq X_{n_i}\), the law is reduced to \(\{x\}\cap\{y\}=\{x,y\}\) by the finite-element calculation \(\{\uparrow X\}=\uparrow\uparrow X\), and the retract is identified with \(D\cong D^\dagger\trianglelefteq\mathbb{P}D\). `PDsingletonApproxMap` is the approximable singleton, `PDsingletonApproxMap_toElementMap` shows it sends \(x\) to \(\{x\}\), and `PDsingleton_principal` is the finite-element identity in the type-correct form \(\{\uparrow X\}=\uparrow(\downarrow X)\), where \(\downarrow X=\{Y\mid Y\subseteq X\}\) is Definition 7.9's token and the outer arrow is the principal filter in the power domain. `PDsingleton_mono` says \(X'\subseteq X\) makes \(\{\uparrow X\}\) lie below \(\{\uparrow X'\}\), the inclusion-reversing direction of Definition 1.7. `PDsingletonApproxMap_rel_Ypd_iff` is the code form \(\exists b\in\mathrm{decodeList}\,k.\, X_n\subseteq X_b\), which is the subset reading of Scott's existential (a source neighbourhood lies in the down-set of some generator), and `singleton_isComputable` decides it by swapping the inclusion decider under a bounded existential. The binary law is `PDfinJoin_pair`: \(\{x,y\}\) equals the meet `PDsingletonMeet` of the two singletons, proved by `PDext` unpacking a two-component family of neighbourhoods into the two singleton witnesses, not by the calculation \(\uparrow\uparrow X\cap\uparrow\uparrow Y=\uparrow(\uparrow X\cup\uparrow Y)\); `PDfinJoin_inter_two` is the membership form of that equality. `ext_of_principal` records that approximable maps agreeing on principal elements are equal, but no induction produces the \(n\)-ary intersection or the \(n\)-ary map. `PDfinJoinApproxMap₂` and `finJoinMap_prod` are the binary approximable map, and `PDfinJoinApproxMap₂_isComputable` shows its relation on the product presentation is the conjunction of two singleton tests. Against the retract, `powerDomain_hasTop` uses unconditional intersection closure, so `improperTop`, the filter of all power-domain neighbourhoods, is a greatest element by `hasTop_of_inter_closed`; `subsystem_inter_closed` and `hasTop_of_iso` transport that top along a subdomain embedding, giving `hasTop_of_trianglelefteq_powerDomain`. `Vshape` has neighbourhoods \(\{\mathrm{true},\mathrm{false}\}\), \(\{\mathrm{true}\}\), and \(\{\mathrm{false}\}\); `ft_inter` and `tf_inter` say the two singletons meet emptily, `Vshape_not_mem_empty` says the empty set is not a neighbourhood, and `Vshape_not_hasTop` concludes there is no greatest element, so `vshape_not_trianglelefteq_powerDomain` refutes \(Vshape\trianglelefteq\mathbb{P}(Vshape)\). `theorem_712` conjoins that negation with the binary action, its computability, the binary intersection law, the code form of the singleton, and `PDsingleton_principal`. The embedding \(D^\dagger\trianglelefteq\mathbb{P}D\) is not proved. **Theorem:** partial — the singleton and the binary join are approximable and computable and \(\{x,y\}=\{x\}\cap\{y\}\) holds, but the statement is \(n\)-ary and \(D\trianglelefteq\mathbb{P}D\) fails for the flat two-point domain. **Proof:** partial — the code inclusion and the binary extensionality argument are present, while Scott's \(n\)-ary finite-element calculation and \(D\cong D^\dagger\trianglelefteq\mathbb{P}D\) are not, and the retract argument is replaced by the top-element obstruction.
